# Java Inheritance
Inheritance is one of the key features of OOP that allows us to create a new class from a existing class.

THe new class that is created is known as sub-class(child or derived class) and the existing class from where the child class is derived is known as superclass(parent or base class).

Class A(Parent)   <----- Class B(child)

we use extends keyword relation between A & B

## Method Overriding
If a subclass provides the specific implementation of the method that has been declared by one of its parent class, it is known as method overriding .
. Method overriding is also known as runtime polymorphism . Hence we can achieve Polymorphism in java with help of Inheritance.


## super  keyword in Java

super is a special keyword in java that is used to refer to the instance of the immediate parent class.
Used of super Keyword in java
=> Uses of super Keyword in java
* It is use to refer to an instance variable if the immediate parent
  class.
* It is used to invoke a method of the immediate parent class.
* It is used to invoke a constructor of immediate parent class.

# this vs super

1. this is an implicit reference variable keyword used to represent the current class.

super is am implicit reference variable keyword used to represent the immediate parent class.

2. this is to invoke methods of the current class

super is used to invoke methods of the immediate parent class.

3. this is used to invoke a constructor of the current class.

super is used to invoke a constructor of the immediate parent class.

4. this refers to the instance and static variables to the current class

super refers to the instance and static variable of immediate parent class.

5. this can be used to return and pass as an argument in the context of current class object.

super can be used to return and pass as an argument in the context of an immediate parent class object.


##  this in breaking() Method

   Car breaking() {
       return this;
   }
   this refers to the current instance of the Car class.

   When breaking() is called, it returns the same Car object on which it was called.

   This allows method chaining, meaning you can do:

   obj.breaking().start();

   Here, breaking() returns the obj itself, so you can call .start() on the returned object.

   2. this in start() Method

   void start() {
       Scooter scooterobj = new Scooter();
       scooterobj.breaking(this);  // Passing the current object
       super.start();
       System.out.println(this);
       System.out.println(this.model + " Car is starting");
   }
   Where this is used:
   scooterobj.breaking(this);

   Here, this is passed as an argument to Scooter's breaking(Car car) method.

   Since this represents the current Car object, it allows Scooter to access its properties (like model).
   System.out.println(this);

   This prints the reference of the current object.
   If toString() is not overridden, it will print the memory address of the object.
   System.out.println(this.model + " Car is starting");

   this.model ensures that the model being accessed belongs to the current Car object.
   This is useful when there's a naming conflict with a local variable (not in this case, but in general).
   3. this in Constructor

   Car() {
       super(3);
       System.out.println("Car is being created");
   }
   Although this is not explicitly used here, it is implicitly present.
   When you create an object:

   Car obj = new Car();
   The constructor refers to the current instance (this) being initialized.


   Why Use this in This Code?
   To pass the current object (this) to another method (scooterobj.breaking(this)).

   To return the current object (this in breaking() for method chaining).
   To distinguish between instance variables and local variables (though not used here).

Final Summary
Usage of this	Purpose

 scooterobj.breaking(this);	Passes the current object (Car) to Scooter's method
   return this;	Returns the current object for method chaining
   System.out.println(this);	Prints the reference of the current object
   this.model	Ensures model belongs to the current object
   Hope this clears your doubts!


 The statement you are asking about means that if you need to call one constructor from another constructor of the same class, or invoke a method from another method, you use the this keyword. Let's break it down.

1. Invoking a Constructor from Another Constructor
In Java, you can use this() to call a different constructor from within the same class.

Example: Calling One Constructor from Another

class Car {
    String model;
    int wheels;

    // First constructor
    Car() {
        this("Default Model", 4);  // Calls the second constructor
        System.out.println("Default Car Constructor");
    }

    // Second constructor
    Car(String model, int wheels) {
        this.model = model;
        this.wheels = wheels;
        System.out.println("Car with model: " + model + " and wheels: " + wheels);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car obj = new Car();
    }
}

Car with model: Default Model and wheels: 4
Default Car Constructor

How this() Works Here?
In the default constructor (Car()), we call this("Default Model", 4);
This calls the parameterized constructor (Car(String model, int wheels)) before executing the rest of Car().
This helps avoid code duplication.


2. Calling a Method from Another Method using this
You can also use this to call another method of the same class.

Example: Calling a Method Using this
class Bike {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Bike is starting...");
        this.accelerate();  // Calls the accelerate() method
    }

    void accelerate() {
        System.out.println("Bike is accelerating...");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bike obj = new Bike();
        obj.start();
    }
}

Bike is starting...
Bike is accelerating...

How this Works Here?
this.accelerate(); calls the accelerate() method from within start().
We could just write accelerate();, but using this explicitly clarifies that the method belongs to the current object.

final Keyword in java
In java, the final keyword is a non-access modifier tht is used to define entities that cannot be changed or modified

Final Variable
Variable with final keyword cannot be assigned again

Final Method
Method with final keyword cannot be overridden by its subClasses

Final Class
Class with final keyword cannot be extended or inherited from other classes